# HQL Book

A small question can require a surprising amount of machinery. The orders
are in a database, the corrections arrive as events, and the explanation
for an exception is in somebody's notes. Before we can ask what happened,
we write a query, a script, and a little program to reconcile their answers.
The little program tends to become permanent.

HQL begins with this everyday inconvenience. It is a statically typed
language for querying and transforming data in records, tables, files,
streams, and vaults of linked Markdown documents. Its central device is a
pipeline: each step receives a value and produces another, and the types
describe how the steps fit together. The program is checked before it runs;
data arriving from outside is checked at the boundary. These checks cannot
tell us whether we asked a sensible question. They can save us from
debugging an answer to a question the program could never ask correctly.

Documents deserve a place in this arrangement. A note has text, but it may
also have a date, an author, links, and claims about other notes. We can
query that structure, follow the links, and put the results back into a
document. When a language model enters the picture, the same concern for
explicit inputs and inspectable results remains. The later chapters explore
a design in which context, prompts, and runs become values that programs
can work with. Generating a program cheaply makes understanding what it
does more valuable.

This book develops these ideas through programs small enough to examine.
Read it in order on a first visit: expressions lead to types, types to
pipelines, and pipelines to systems whose parts need not live in the same
place. Run the examples, change an input, and try to predict the result
before asking the machine. A type error is a useful result of this exercise.

The five parts follow that progression. **The language** introduces values,
functions, queries, types, and errors. **Data flows** connects checked
pipelines to files, tables, topics, and other systems. **Knowledge and AI**
turns to documents, graphs, retrieval, and the design of agents and connected
vaults. **The standard library** supplies statistical, random,
encoding, and cryptographic operations. **Extending HQL** shows how
functions implemented in Rust become libraries that HQL programs can import.

## Following along

[Install `hql`](../install.md), clone the repository for the reading-club vault,
and run each program with `hql eval`:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/ewiger/hql && cd hql
hql --vault examples/reading-club eval 'cards | size'
```

The reading-club vault, [`examples/reading-club/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/examples/reading-club),
is a small reading club: seven books, five authors, the links between them,
one claim about which book inspired which, and a librarian.

The programs that reach a server — Kafka, PostgreSQL, NATS, a lakehouse —
run in the [hql-demo repository](https://github.com/ewiger/hql-demo). Each
of its cases is a vault with the Docker Compose file that starts what it
needs and a `run.sh` that compares the output with the expected one. With
Docker and `hql` on the `PATH`:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/ewiger/hql-demo && cd hql-demo
./run-all.sh
```

The book distinguishes the implemented language from its design direction.
Chapter 18 and chapters 25 to 27 describe APIs and object stores, the `ai`
module, and the direction beyond one vault; these are design, not features
of the current release. The marked sections of chapters 14, 15, 19 and 37
also describe design. Chapter 14's companion on planning separates the local
backend from future execution strategies. Runnable examples show their
expected output; the programs in chapters 16, 17, 19 and 20 that reach a
server are checked in the book and executed in the
[hql-demo repository](https://github.com/ewiger/hql-demo).

## Part I: The language

0. [[00-ai-oriented-language|An AI-oriented language]] —  What an AI-oriented
   programming language could look like? What properties it should have?
   And where one goes with it when it comes to mixing knowledge, natural
   language, with both structured and unstructured data. As well as evidence
   of what is true and false, and how to reason about things in an almost
   precise logical way.
1. [[01-hello-world|Hello world]] — the first program, running it, and
   the type check before a program runs.
2. [[02-data-types|Data types]] — `Int`, `Float`, `String` and
   `Bool` in the REPL, `Bytes`, `Data` and its JSON-shaped trees, `Option`
   for a value that may be missing, and how sets, `Data` and maps differ.
3. [[03-collections|Collections]] — lists, sets and maps, and the steps they
   share.
4. [[04-functions|Functions]] — lambdas, declared functions, the
   two body forms `= expr` and `{ … }`, generic functions, aliases, and
   partial application.
5. [[05-modules|Modules]] — modules of your own, imports, receiver
   modules, public and private declarations, how a name resolves, and the
   packages modules come in.
6. [[06-sql-like-queries|SQL-like Queries]] — where, order by, group by and the
   aggregates.
7. [[07-streams|Streams that do not end]] — generators, laziness, and
   batches.
8. [[08-output|Writing results out]] — presentation formats and who chooses
   them.
9. [[09-io|I/O]] — sources and targets: endpoints, `source`, `tap`,
   `target` and `sink`, finite or live, formats, endpoints by id and the schema
   boundary, channels, logging, the network, files and folders, the
   environment and arguments, and `hql` as a command-line data tool.
10. [[10-the-type-system|The type system]] — records, unions and
    match, results, subtypes and narrowing, generics, receiver calls,
    operators and traits.
11. [[11-advanced-data-typing|Advanced data typing]] — the type
    boundary where loose data becomes typed, open and closed types,
    `TypeContract` and `DataContract`, validators, validation as a value,
    and what a contract states that a schema cannot.
12. [[12-errors|Errors]] — failures kept as data, diagnostics and the
    run's diagnostic channel, `strict` and `collect`.
13. [Working with `hql`](13-working-with-hql.hmd) — the commands, the reports,
    and what to do when something is wrong.

## Part II: Data flows

14. [[14-etl-pipelines|ETL pipelines]] — the wiring as a program: `|`
    and `.`, the three phases, logical steps and effects, `hql`
    in a shell pipe, the shop whose orders the rest of Part II follows, and
    partitioning, schedules and backends. Its companion,
    [[14-planning-and-backends|Logical steps and physical plans]], follows
    a checked program into local execution and the design direction for
    parallel workers, SQL translation and distributed execution.
15. [[15-endpoints|Endpoints]] — one model for every system: a file by
    path or by id, `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink`, the kind that states
    the type, secrets in the environment, and transports.
16. [[16-tables-and-topics|Tables and topics]] — the shop's orders in a
    SQLite table, a PostgreSQL table and a Kafka topic, what a write
    promises, and a topic whose messages are not all orders.
17. [[17-subjects-and-the-lakehouse|Subjects and the lakehouse]] — core
    NATS notifications, a JetStream log and a durable consumer, and bronze,
    silver and gold Delta tables.
18. [[18-apis-webhooks-and-stores|APIs, webhooks and stores]] — a paginated
    REST API, a GraphQL catalogue, a webhook, an archive in object storage,
    and the systems the transport families reserve.
19. [[19-oltp|OLTP]] — the shop's transactional side: its whole day
    in one checked file, where its mistakes are found, a replay as the same
    file, and streams against tables.
20. [[20-olap|OLAP]] — the history those transactions leave: bronze,
    silver and gold, the grain of a row and its keys, deduplication, a table
    kept up to date by key, and incremental loads.

## Part III: Knowledge and AI

21. [[21-a-vault|A vault of documents]] — cards, their metadata, a reading
    list nobody maintains, and the vault as a namespace.
22. [[22-graphs|Links and graphs]] — following links, building graphs,
    drawing them.
23. [[23-retrieval|Finding cards by meaning]] — rankings, hits, and the
    evidence behind a score.
24. [[24-documents|Queries inside documents]] — cells, documents built
    from them, and cells that maintain a vault.
25. [[25-agents-and-runs|Agents and runs]] — agents as cards, context and
    prompts, tools, and runs kept as values.
26. [[26-agents-inside-documents|Agents inside documents]] — a document
    whose cells retrieve evidence, run an agent over the draft they sit in,
    and remember the answer.
27. [[27-the-hyper-web|The hyper web]] — namespaces beyond one tree,
    providers, retrieval and agents across them, and a reader who asks.

## Part IV: The standard library

28. [[28-statistics|Statistics]] — means, spread, quantiles, the mode and
    correlation, over a list and over a query's column.
29. [[29-random|Random values]] — sampling, shuffling, choice, numbers and
    UUIDs, and a seed that makes them reproducible.
30. [[30-digests-and-encodings|Digests and encodings]] — digests of text
    and bytes, the fingerprint of a `Data` tree, and hex, base64 and the
    other text encodings.
31. [[31-cryptography|Keys and cryptography]] — keys and their formats,
    secrets that print redacted, signatures, MACs and password hashes, and
    the encryption and key vault that are design.

## Part V: Extending HQL

32. [[32-functions-and-declarations|Functions and declarations]] — a
    function with a body in HQL, a declaration whose body is Rust, and how
    the standard library is built from the second.
33. [[33-packages-and-extensions|Packages and extensions]] — an `import`
    followed from the module it names to the package, the extension and
    the file that runs.
34. [[34-writing-an-extension|Writing an extension]] — a package from an
    empty directory: the crate, the contract, the Rust functions, values,
    errors, and functions passed in.
35. [[35-how-a-library-is-loaded|How a library is loaded]] — static and
    dynamic libraries, the one symbol `hql` calls, and why a library fits
    one `hql` and one compiler.
36. [[36-installing-packages|Installing packages]] — `hql package`, what
    an installed package is, a package under development, and the
    settings of `.hql/config.toml`.
37. [[37-the-registry-and-publishing|The registry and publishing]] —
    official and known packages, version ranges and exact versions, and
    publishing a crate.
38. [[38-the-ml-package|The ml package]] — datasets, models as values,
    prediction and metrics, from the first official package.
